-- =====================================================================
-- Drop the loan_repayments -> expenses half of the circular reference
-- =====================================================================
-- `loan_repayments.expenseId` and `expenses.loanRepaymentId` pointed at each
-- other. That cycle is why both foreign keys were created
-- `DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED` by migration 20261017000600, and why Prisma
-- (which cannot express a mutual reference) emitted `DROP CONSTRAINT` for both
-- in every autogenerated migration.
--
-- The link is now one-directional: `Expense.loanRepaymentId` is a real Prisma
-- relation mapped onto the existing `fk_expenses_loan_repayment` name, and a
-- repayment's expense is read through the reverse side of that relation.
--
-- `loan_repayments` was confirmed EMPTY on both the dev and test databases
-- before this migration ran, so dropping the column loses no rows. Both FK
-- columns also held no values (`expenses.loanRepaymentId` was null throughout).
--
-- All three statements use `IF EXISTS` on purpose. The very first attempt to
-- apply this migration against dev failed on the (unquoted) index name —
-- Postgres folded `loan_repayments_expenseId_idx` to lower-case and reported
-- "index does not exist" — after the constraint drop had already executed.
-- Prisma recorded the migration as FAILED (`finished_at` NULL, `applied_steps`
-- 0), so it was rolled back with `prisma migrate resolve --rolled-back` and is
-- being re-applied with this corrected, idempotent file. The `IF EXISTS`
-- clauses let it finish cleanly on dev (constraint already gone) and apply
-- normally on test (nothing removed yet). It only ever removes these three
-- objects; it cannot mask a different mistake.

ALTER TABLE loan_repayments
  DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS fk_loan_repayments_expense;

DROP INDEX IF EXISTS "loan_repayments_expenseId_idx";

ALTER TABLE loan_repayments
  DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS "expenseId";
